/* Odyssey HUD — the approved interface specification, 2026-09-16.
 *
 * WHAT THIS SHEET IS AND IS NOT ALLOWED TO DO
 *
 * It owns colour, space, radius, borders and state. It owns no type at all: every font size,
 * weight and tracking in the HUD comes from `HudType` through `HudText`, in the Unity-free
 * Odyssey.Hud assembly, because "six sizes and no others" is an acceptance criterion and a size
 * written here could only be tested by parsing this file. There is deliberately not one
 * `font-size` below.
 *
 * Nor does it own the position of a region. Regions are anchored to screen edges or centred by
 * the layout engine, and `HudLayout` is the arithmetic that says where that puts them — which is
 * how "no two panels overlap at 1280x720, 1920x1080 or 2560x1440" became a test in the fast tier
 * rather than an opinion in a review. The offsets below are the anchors themselves (left:20,
 * right:76, bottom:84) and they are the same numbers HudLayout holds; HudStyleSheetTests fails
 * the tier if the two disagree.
 *
 * Colours are literal rather than var(--x), which is the position this sheet has always taken and
 * is worth restating: the sheet stays legible as plain text and there are no custom-property
 * resolution surprises across panel copies. What has changed is that the literals are now checked
 * — HudStyleSheetTests parses the declarations named in its own table and compares them against
 * HudTheme, so a colour nobody agreed to cannot reach the screen.
 *
 * ONE THING IN THE SPEC THIS ENGINE CANNOT DO. The panel fill is specified as
 * rgba(12,16,20,.86) plus backdrop-filter: blur(6px). UI Toolkit has no backdrop filter and no
 * way to sample what is behind an element, so the blur is simply absent. The fill is unchanged;
 * what is lost is the softening of bright terrain seen through a panel. The two scrims carry the
 * contrast that mattered, so the omission costs legibility nothing — see HudContrast, which
 * measures it.
 */

/* Unity's runtime theme gives every Label a small vertical margin of its own. Inside a row of a
   fixed height that costs nothing, which is why it went unnoticed: the stores rows, the clock line
   and the inspect header all size their own children. It shows up on a label that is the last
   thing in a panel — the depth rail's "R / F" hint was adding two pixels to the rail's height, and
   the empty inspect pane's one line would have done the same. Reset first, so that every margin
   below is one this sheet asked for. */
.unity-label {
    margin: 0;
}

.hud {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;

    /* The HUD's ink, inherited by every label that does not name its own.

       This is a fix for a whole class of bug rather than for the one that found it (owner,
       2026-09-17: "I couldn't see any text on the save as/save buttons when saving a world").
       `color` is an inherited property in UI Toolkit, and nothing in this sheet set one above a
       leaf — so a label built without a style class fell through to the imported default runtime
       theme, whose ink is dark, and drew invisibly on a panel that is nearly black. Every label in
       the HUD passes a class and so nobody had been bitten; the moment two did not, they vanished
       silently rather than looking wrong, which is the worst way for a mistake like this to
       present.

       Two labels were already in that state and had been for some time: the naming prompt's
       answers, and "MADE OF" on the Build palette's material row. Setting the inherited ink here
       means the next one is merely unstyled instead of absent. Every class below still overrides
       it, so nothing that already had a colour changes.

       HudStyleSheetTests pins it, because a default nobody can see the absence of is exactly the
       kind that gets deleted as unused. */
    color: #eef3f6;

    /* The shell must not catch the pointer anywhere a region does not sit, or the world would be
       unclickable behind a full-screen element. Set from code (PickingMode.Ignore), where it
       cannot be lost with a stylesheet swap. */
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- scrims

   Always on, both of them, and never a pointer target. They are the reason the panels could
   shrink: a panel dark enough to hold 13 px text over bright terrain has to be nearly opaque, and
   a screen of nearly opaque panels is the coverage this rebuild was asked to halve. The ramp
   itself is a texture built in HudTokens.VerticalRamp, because USS has no gradient property. */

.scrim {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; right: 0;
    background-size: 100% 100%;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- panels */

.panel {
    position: absolute;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.86);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 5px;
    padding: 12px;
}

.panel__hdr {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    height: 20px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* Every label that shares a column with another declares its height and centres its text in it.
   Left to itself a label claims a line box of about twice the point size — 26 px for 13 px type —
   and two of those stacked inside a 38 px header overlap the tabs under them, which is exactly
   what the first screenshot of this HUD showed. An explicit box with `middle` alignment centres
   the ink inside it, so the leading falls outside the box instead of pushing the next row down. */
.panel__label {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    white-space: nowrap;
    height: 20px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.panel__hdrright {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- shared bits */

.bar {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
    border-radius: 2px;
    overflow: hidden;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bar__fill {
    height: 100%;
    background-color: #7fc98c;
    border-radius: 2px;
}

/* The bar under a need in the inspect pane. */
.bar--need { height: 4px; margin-top: 4px; }

/* An explicit height, because HudLayout.BuildCatRows is a count of these and a row whose height
   is content-driven cannot be counted. 30 px box plus 2 px of margin each side is the 34 px pitch
   the model works in. */
.chip {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 30px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 4px 8px;
    margin: 2px;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.chip__label { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); }
.chip:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }
.chip--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.chip--off:hover { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13); }

/* Armed. The accent the rest of the interface uses for "this is on", so a lit palette chip
   and a lit command-bar item say the same thing. */
.chip--on { border-color: #6fd3e3; background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.12); }
.chip--on .chip__label { color: #dff6fa; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A1 stores */

/* Docked to the top-left corner (owner, 2026-09-17), so it lines up with the colonist strip
   beside it, which has been at the very top since the bars were docked. The corners that sit on
   a screen edge are squared, for the same reason the command bar's are: a rounded corner against
   the edge shows a notch of world through it. The breathing room moved inside — the panel's own
   12 px padding is untouched, so no text is nearer the edge than it was. */
.stores {
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 168px;
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 0;
}

.stores__count {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    margin-right: 6px;
    height: 20px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

.stores__rows { margin-top: 9px; }

.stores__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 29px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0 4px;
}

.stores__row:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05); }

.stores__name {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.stores__value {
    color: #eef3f6;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

/* A commodity the colony has none of. Folded away behind the disclosure by default; at 0.42 it is
   still readable when it is shown, which is what the disclosure is for. */
.stores__row--zero { opacity: 0.42; }

/* Falling: an amber down-chevron before the value, and the faintest tint across the row. */
.stores__row--falling { background-color: rgba(232, 181, 92, 0.09); }
.stores__falling { margin-right: 5px; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A2 colonist strip */

/* Docked to the very top (owner, 2026-09-17) and allowed to wrap to a second row. The left and
   right insets are written from code on every resize, because what the strip may occupy is twice
   the smaller of its two clearances rather than the whole screen — and a wrap has to happen at
   that width, not at the screen's. */
.strip {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; right: 0;
    top: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    align-content: flex-start;
}

/* Two rows and nothing else: who this is, and what they are doing. The three need bars came off
   on 2026-09-17 (owner), and with them went most of the card's height — a card is a way of
   finding somebody and saying what they are at, and the strip is the densest region on the
   screen, so every pixel it does not spend buys another colonist on the bar. */
.card {
    width: 126px;
    height: 89px;
    /* Half the gap on each side horizontally, the whole of it below — so the gap between two
       rows is one CardGap and the first row still starts exactly at the strip's top edge, which
       is what lets the layout model state the strip's height as rows and gaps and be right. */
    margin: 0 3.5px 7px 3.5px;
    padding: 8px;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.86);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 5px;
}

.card:hover { border-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.55); }
.card--sel { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

/* The spec's "0 0 0 1px rgba(111,211,227,.35)" outside the accent border. UI Toolkit has no
   box-shadow, so the ring is an element inset past the card's own edge. */
.card__ring {
    position: absolute;
    left: -2px; top: -2px; right: -2px; bottom: -2px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.35);
    border-radius: 6px;
}

.card__top {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

.card__avatar {
    width: 52px;
    height: 52px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.card__names {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 8px;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The one place in the HUD where text is allowed to be cut short: a colonist's own name, which
   can be any length and belongs on a card of a fixed width. */
.card__name {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
    height: 19px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* Its own line, the full width of the card rather than the strip beside the avatar, and therefore
   not truncated. The acceptance criteria allow an ellipsis on a colonist's name and on nothing
   else, so the job label has to actually fit: 116 px holds every job this game has at 12 px, with
   room for several more — 93 px of it since the icon took the left of the line, which still holds
   the longest word the game has by a wide margin. */
.card__jobrow {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 17px;
}

.card__job {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    white-space: nowrap;
    height: 17px;
    margin-left: 6px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A3/A4 clock and speed,
                                                                     A5 alerts

   One column, not two corners. The clock and the alerts were absolutely positioned with
   hand-picked tops until the clock grew past the 122 px the alerts panel's fixed top allowed it
   and buried the speed buttons. Heights are content's business; only the order is ours. */

/* Docked to the top edge with the strip and the stores panel (owner, 2026-09-17): the clock sat
   twenty pixels below a strip beside it that started at zero, and the two read as misaligned
   because they were. It is not against the right edge — the depth rail is, and this column sits
   inside it — so only its top corners are squared. */
.column-right {
    position: absolute;
    right: 56px;
    top: 0;
    width: 266px;
}

.clock.panel {
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
}

.clock { position: relative; width: 100%; }

.clock__line {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    height: 28px;
}

.clock__time {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 28px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.clock__date {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    height: 28px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

.speed {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 9px;
    height: 26px;
}

.speed__btn {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    height: 26px;
    margin-right: 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
    border-radius: 4px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.speed__btn:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

.speed__btn--on {
    background-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
}

.alerts {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

/* The negative bottom margin cancels the last row's own gap, so the panel is exactly
   rows * height + (rows - 1) * gap tall — which is what HudLayout models and what the PlayMode
   gate compares the realised box against. The same trick is used by the needs grid and the
   depth rail below. */
.alerts__rows { margin-top: 9px; margin-bottom: -7px; }

/* Two lines of 13 px type at the renderer's own line height, which is nearer 26 px a line than
   the 17 the point size suggests. A minimum rather than a fixed height: a one-line alert still
   occupies the modelled row so the panel's height is predictable, and a longer one grows rather
   than being clipped. The authored leads and details are all one or two lines in a 242 px
   column. */
.alert {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 7px;
    min-height: 52px;
}

.alert__icon { margin-top: 1px; }

.alert__text {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
}

/* Lead first in full ink, detail trailing dim. A player who reads only the leads should know
   what to do; the detail is there for the one they stop on. */
.alert__lead {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: normal;
}

.alert__detail {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.60);
    white-space: normal;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the right-hand gutter

   One column against the right edge holding the depth rail and, under it, the orders strip
   (owner, 2026-09-17: "a vertical button strip that sits below the depth control … very close to
   the screen border"). A column rather than two anchored panels because the rail is the one
   region the WORLD sizes — its cells shrink to fit the screen, so its height is not a number
   anyone can write down here — and a strip given a top of its own would sit under a rail of one
   particular length and float away from every other. This is the same fix, for the same reason,
   as the clock-and-alerts column: never two panels in one corner with hand-picked tops. */

.column-edge {
    position: absolute;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 44px;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A11 depth rail

   Four pixels of side padding rather than twelve, because the spec asks for 26 px cells in a
   44 px rail and twelve either side would leave twenty. See HudLayout.RailSidePad. */

.rail {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 4px;
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
}

.rail .panel__hdr {
    height: 14px;
    justify-content: center;
}

.rail__cells {
    margin-top: 6px;
    margin-bottom: -6px;
    align-items: center;
}

.rail__cell {
    width: 26px;
    height: 16px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35);
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.rail__cell:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18); }

/* Above the surface is sky, and sky is pale. Below it is rock, and rock is dark. That contrast is
   the one thing a depth readout has to say at a glance. */
.rail__cell--above { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

.rail__cell--active {
    background-color: #6fd3e3;
}

.rail__cell--surface { border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35); }

.rail__number {
    color: #0b1116;
    height: 16px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.rail__dot {
    width: 4px;
    height: 4px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    background-color: #e8b55c;
}

.rail__hint {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    height: 14px;
    margin-top: 6px;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the orders strip

   The four verbs a player applies to what is already on the board, in the gutter under the rail.
   No label row: "DEPTH" fits 44 px and "ORDERS" does not, and the criteria forbid clipped text —
   every button says what it is in a tooltip instead, as these did when they were 26 px squares in
   the Build palette's header. Six at the top rather than twelve, because a strip with no text in
   it would otherwise spend a quarter of its height on air — and none at the bottom, because the
   last button's own margin is already there. UI Toolkit has no :last-child to cancel it with, and
   a padding that double-counts a gap is how a model and a sheet come to disagree by six pixels.
   See HudLayout.Orders*. */

.orders {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: 9px;
    padding-top: 6px;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 4px;
    align-items: center;
    border-top-right-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
}

/* The fill and the border are written from code, in the order's own hue (HudTheme.PinnedActionHue)
   and doubled while it is held — the same treatment, and the same four tokens, the open palette
   wears along its top edge while that order is in the player's hand. */
.ord__btn {
    width: 34px;
    height: 34px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.ord__btn:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A9/A10 inspect */

/* Docked to the left edge with the stores panel above it. Its bottom is not on a screen edge —
   it clears the command bar — so only the two left corners are squared. */
.inspect {
    left: 0;
    bottom: 64px;
    width: 560px;
    border-top-left-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-left-radius: 0;
}

/* The tile readout and a selected pile stand in a column, not a band: half the width, with the
   facts in rows instead of a sentence (owner, 2026-09-17 — the same fact is always in the same
   place now, where the joined line made every number hunt for its label). */
.inspect--narrow {
    width: 280px;
}

/* One fact of the readout: the label in a fixed-width column, the value beside it. The label
   column is a width rather than a gap so the values line up whatever the labels are. */
.inspect__rows { margin-top: 2px; }

.inspect__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 20px;
}

.inspect__rowname {
    width: 92px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.inspect__rowvalue {
    flex-grow: 1;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* There is no empty state. With nothing selected the pane is display:none and leaves the layout
   (owner, 2026-09-16); the ".inspect__empty" rule that used to sit here styled a line reading
   "Nothing selected", which is the interface talking about itself. */

.inspect__hdr {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-start;
    height: 60px;
}

/* A tile or a pile keeps the shorter header: its slot holds an icon at one of the interface's
   three sizes, not a 60 px photograph of a person. */
.inspect--narrow .inspect__hdr {
    height: 38px;
}

/* No overflow rule: clipping is what the acceptance criteria forbid, and the ellipsis they do
   allow is for a colonist's name on a 132 px card, not for a 560 px pane. */
.inspect__titles {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
}

.inspect__nameline {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 22px;
}

.inspect__title {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    height: 22px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.inspect__meta {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    margin-left: 9px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    height: 22px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

/* Twenty-two and sixteen come to the thirty-eight the layout model gives the header. */
.inspect__state {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    white-space: nowrap;
    height: 16px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.inspect__actions {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.action {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 26px;
    padding: 0 9px;
    margin-left: 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.action:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }
.action--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.action--off:hover { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13); }
.action__label { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); margin-left: 6px; }

.inspect__close {
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
    margin-left: 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

.inspect__close:hover { border-color: #e06a5c; }

.inspect__tabs {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 6px;
    height: 26px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
}

.tab {
    height: 24px;
    padding: 0 10px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    border-radius: 3px 3px 0 0;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.tab--on {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.12);
    color: #6fd3e3;
    border-bottom-width: 2px;
    border-bottom-color: #6fd3e3;
}

.tab--off { opacity: 0.42; }

/* Two columns, 9 px between rows and 18 px between columns. The old pane stacked them, which is
   why it was 690 px wide and 302 tall for three numbers. */
/* The colonist body's tab area: one height, whatever tab is showing.

   The pane is docked to its bottom edge and grows upward, so a body that sized itself to its own
   tab moved the header, the tab strip and every row under the pointer each time the player
   changed tab — Needs is two rows of 25 against Skills' seven of 19, ninety-eight pixels apart
   (owner, 2026-09-18: "it resizes every time … it needs to be at least a fixed size"). The
   tallest live tab sets the height here; a shorter one sits at the top of it with the slack
   below, so between tabs only the rows change. HudLayout.InspectTabBody is the same number and
   HudStyleSheetTests holds the two together. */
.inspect__tabbody {
    height: 157px;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

.needs {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;

    /* Eighteen pixels of room borrowed back, so that two 256 px columns and the gap between them
       fit the pane's 534 px of content with the second column's own trailing margin hanging over
       the edge rather than forcing a wrap. Flex counts a trailing margin when it decides whether
       a line has room, which is what made the first version of this grid one column wide. */
    margin-right: -18px;
    margin-bottom: -9px;
}

.need {
    width: 256px;
    margin-bottom: 9px;
    margin-right: 18px;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- B1 Skills tab

   The same two-column grid as the needs, for the same reason: thirteen skills in one column
   would make the pane 280 px taller than the screen has to spare over the command bar, and would
   leave half of a 560 px pane empty while doing it. The negative trailing margins are the .needs
   trick above and carry the same explanation. */
.skills {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-right: -18px;
    margin-bottom: -4px;
}

.skill {
    width: 256px;
    height: 19px;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    margin-right: 18px;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

/* A skill with no system behind it yet. Present and readable rather than hidden — the panel
   catalogue's "disabled with a reason" rule — but visibly not a number you have. */
.skill--off { opacity: 0.45; }

.skill__name {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.skill__level {
    width: 22px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

/* Two lozenges rather than the word "major": thirteen rows of words is a column nobody reads,
   and what the player wants from this list is its shape at a glance. */
.skill__passion {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    width: 18px;
    margin-left: 7px;
}

.skill__pip {
    width: 5px;
    height: 5px;
    margin-right: 3px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background-color: #e8b55c;
}

.need__line {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 17px;
}

.need__name {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 8px;
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 17px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.need__value {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    height: 17px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

.inspect--tomb { opacity: 0.55; }
.inspect__reason { color: #e8b55c; margin-top: 6px; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A8 command bar

   The bar may not wrap and may not overflow. Anything that does not fit goes into Menu, which is
   what lets the inspect pane above it stop guessing at the bar's height — a coupling the old
   sheet carried in a comment admitting it was one. */

/* Docked to the very bottom (owner, 2026-09-17), for the same reason the strip is docked to the
   very top: a bar with a strip of world under it reads as floating rather than as the edge of
   the screen, and the twenty pixels are worth more as board. */
.bar-row {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
}

/* The full width of the screen (owner, 2026-09-17), so no corner is rounded and the only edge
   drawn is the top one: three of its four sides are off the screen. It is the edge of the screen
   rather than a panel floating near it, and a popover raised from a button on it has somewhere
   definite to sit. */
.commandbar {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 5px;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.90);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 0;
    border-left-width: 0;
    border-right-width: 0;
    border-bottom-width: 0;
}

.cmd {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 38px;
    padding: 0 12px;
    margin-right: 3px;
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.cmd:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

.cmd__label {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
    margin-left: 9px;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.cmd__key {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
    margin-left: 8px;
}

/* Build, the one item on the row that is a mode rather than a command.

   IT WAS FILLED ALL THE TIME UNTIL 2026-09-17, and that was the bug the owner reported: "the build
   button still stays bold when it shouldn't and is confusing — it's an indicator to whether you
   are truly in build mode". A solid accent fill drawn permanently swamps the .cmd--on wash that
   was supposed to say "open", so the cap looked identical whether the player was placing walls or
   had left the palette three minutes ago.

   So the fill became the *state* and an outline became the resting style: accent border and accent
   ink while nothing is armed, the filled cap the bar always had while build mode is on. Build is
   still the only item on the row with a colour of its own, so it is still as findable as it was —
   what it no longer does is claim to be active when it is not. */
.cmd--primary {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.10);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
    padding: 0 14px;
}

.cmd--primary:hover { background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.20); }
.cmd__label--primary { color: #6fd3e3; }
.cmd--primary .cmd__key { color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.55); }

/* In build mode: the filled cap. */
.cmd--primary.cmd--on { background-color: #6fd3e3; }
.cmd--primary.cmd--on:hover { background-color: #8adfec; }
.cmd--primary.cmd--on .cmd__label--primary { color: #0b1116; }
.cmd--primary.cmd--on .cmd__key { color: rgba(11, 17, 22, 0.55); }

/* A command with nothing behind it yet. 0.42 is the sheet's one off value, shared with
   .chip--off, .menu__row--off, .action--off and .tab--off; the bar used to sit at 0.55 and read as
   merely quiet rather than unavailable. The hover rule cancels .cmd:hover, the way every other
   off state already cancels its own — a control that lights up under the pointer is promising
   something it will not do. */
.cmd--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.cmd--off:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }
.cmd--on { background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.18); }

.commandbar__divider {
    width: 1px;
    height: 26px;
    margin: 0 3px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- menu popup */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- windows and popovers

   A window is a panel you deliberately opened and are looking at, as against a board panel you
   read while watching the world. One rule for all of them (owner, 2026-09-17): less transparent
   than a board panel, a close X in the top right, and closed by Escape. The board behind a window
   is not being read, and showing it through a list of rows is noise on the one surface the player
   is attending to. */
.window {
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.96);
}

/* A window raised from the command bar, which is additionally anchored to the left edge of the
   button that raised it and sits flush on the bar with no gap. The left and bottom are written
   from code, because where a popover sits is a fact about the button that raised it and only the
   laid-out bar knows where that is. Square at the bottom, because that edge meets the bar. */
.popover {
    border-bottom-left-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
}

.menu {
    width: 268px;
    max-height: 560px;
}

.menu__section {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    margin: 8px 0 4px 0;
}

.menu__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 29px;
    padding: 0 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
}

.menu__row:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }
.menu__row--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.menu__row--off:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }

.menu__label {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.menu__key { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35); }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- A7 build palette

   Three layouts over one set of contents, to `docs/design/17-build-palette-layouts.md`.

   WHAT IS NOT IN HERE. Seven category hues and four material tints, each with four states
   derived from it, is forty-four colour declarations for colours that are already arithmetic on
   a token — so those are set inline from HudTheme by HudShell.Build.cs. That is not a literal
   escaping into the Presentation assembly: the tokens are still the only source, and BuildTier
   is what does the deriving. What is left below is the geometry and the neutral states, which
   are the parts a stylesheet is good at and which HudStyleSheetTests can check.

   WHERE THE PANEL SITS. Docked, not floating. The specification drew all three at a 28px margin
   over a bare board; the real screen has stores down the left edge and the roster strip across
   the top. "Tight and flush to other elements to enable full use of space" (owner, 2026-09-17),
   so Rows and Bar span edge to edge and the bottom is written from code, because what is under
   the panel is the command bar or the inspect pane's collapsed header depending on whether
   anything is selected. */

.bp {
    /* Rows and Bar take their left and right from code; Rail takes a width. Square at the
       bottom, because that edge meets whatever it is docked on. */
    border-bottom-left-radius: 0;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
    padding: 0;
}

.bp--rows { width: 372px; }

.bp--rail { width: 840px; }

/* ---- header: BUILD, the mode line, the switcher, ESC, the way out

   One row in all three layouts (owner, 2026-09-17: "shift the toggle view, esc and x onto the same
   row as the build text — this will tidy that up"). It was two stacked lines in Rows, because the
   controls there were eight buttons and with a three-part breadcrumb in front of them the row came
   to 426 px against a 372 px panel. Five of those eight left for the orders strip, and what is
   left fits. */

.bp__hdr {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__hdr-id {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.bp__hdr-ctl {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* The breadcrumb, and the mode line that replaces it while one of the four orders is held.
   Its colour is written from code: cyan for a build, the held order's own hue otherwise.

   It is the part of the header that gives. Rows is 372 px and the longest breadcrumb the content
   table can produce — "Structure > Roof and floor above > Wood" — does not fit beside BUILD and
   five controls at any width this panel has. So the crumb shrinks and ellipsises rather than
   pushing the controls off the end: the same treatment a save's name gets in the load list, and
   the reason the row can be one row at every breadcrumb rather than only at today's. Nothing is
   lost that is not also on the lit tiles directly underneath. */
.bp__crumb {
    margin-left: 9px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    flex-shrink: 1;
}

.bp__mode-icon {
    margin-left: 9px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__cost { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); }

.bp__esc {
    margin-left: 9px;
    margin-right: 9px;
}

/* ---- the layout switcher */

.bp__switch {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__switch-btn {
    width: 22px;
    height: 22px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
    border-radius: 4px;
    margin-left: 3px;
}

.bp__switch-btn:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

.bp__switch-btn--on {
    background-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
}

/* ---- the four actions, and the way out. Their colours are written from code, because each
   one is a different hue and the hue is what says which mode you are in. */

/* The close button, and nothing else since 2026-09-17: the four order buttons that shared this
   class moved to the orders strip in the right-hand gutter. */
.bp__action {
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    margin-left: 6px;
}

.bp__action--close {
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18);
}

.bp__action--close:hover { border-color: #e06a5c; }

/* ---- bands, which is what Rows is made of */

.bp__body { flex-shrink: 0; }

.bp__band {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__divider {
    height: 1px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
    margin-left: 14px;
    margin-right: 14px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* ---- a tile, in every tier and every layout */

.bp__tile {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__tile-label {
    margin-left: 9px;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

/* Neutral tier: the sub-types. The categories and the materials paint themselves from code. */
.bp__sub, .bp__sub-tile, .bp__bar-sub {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14);
}

.bp__sub:hover, .bp__sub-tile:hover, .bp__bar-sub:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.26);
}

.bp__tile--on {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.14);
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
}

.bp__tile--on .bp__tile-label { color: #6fd3e3; }

.bp__tile--off {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);
    cursor: initial;
}

.bp__tile--off:hover {
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07);
}

.bp__tile--off .bp__tile-label { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35); }

/* ---- 4a, Rows, the default

   A column down the left rather than a band across the screen (owner, 2026-09-17): "short width
   as possible but evenly sized … you could probably fit 4 on a row but increase the height and
   try to use the left hand side of the screen instead of the width". Seven tiles stretched across
   1920 were seven very wide tiles with a small icon adrift in each, and they hid the board the
   player is aiming at. */

/* Four equal columns, wrapping — so seven categories stand two rows deep, which is where the
   height comes from. Percentage widths rather than flex-grow, because a growing row does not
   wrap into even columns: 4 x 23% plus a 6px gap after each fits the band's inner width with a
   few pixels to spare. Same construction as Rail's grids, for the same reason. */
.bp__cats {
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: flex-start;
}

.bp__cat {
    height: 62px;
    width: 23%;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-right: 6px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
}

/* An icon over a label, both centred, so the label's own left margin has to come off. */
.bp__cat .bp__tile-label {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-top: 4px;
}

.bp__subs { flex-wrap: wrap; }

/* Fixed, and tall enough for the widest category — Structure, at three rows (owner, 2026-09-17:
   "the height needs to stay fixed … so it can accommodate all of the menus"). The panel is docked
   on the command bar and grows upward, so a category with fewer sub-types than the last one does
   not shrink neatly: it drops the whole control down the screen while the player is aiming at it.
   align-content keeps the rows at the top of the reserved box instead of spreading them through
   it. */
.bp--rows .bp__subs {
    height: 144px;
    align-content: flex-start;
    align-items: flex-start;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* Reserved for the same reason, one tier down: five of the seven categories open on a tool that
   is not made of anything, and a hidden row takes its height with it. */
.bp--rows .bp__mats-row {
    height: 34px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__sub {
    height: 34px;
    padding: 0 13px 0 11px;
    margin-right: 6px;
    margin-top: 3px;
    margin-bottom: 3px;
}

/* The material band stacks: the word, the buttons, then the price. Across a full-width band those
   three sat on one line with the cost pushed to the far right; in a 372px column that line does
   not exist, and a 66px label column in front of two buttons would spend a fifth of the width on
   a word. */
.bp__mats {
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
}

/* The palette's captions take the panel-label ink rather than inheriting whatever is above them.
   That is main's fix of 2026-09-17, ported: the old palette's "MADE OF" had no class at all, so it
   took the imported runtime theme's dark ink and had been invisible since the material row was
   built. `.hud` carries a default ink now, which would have covered these — but a caption that
   says what colour it is cannot be uncovered again by the next theme change. */
.bp__mats-label {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
}

.bp__mats-row {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
}

/* The same box as a sub-type button beside it (owner, 2026-09-17: "evenly sized in font and size
   as the other buttons but keep the style"). The style is the tint, the doubled border and the
   seated shadow, and all of it survives the box and the type coming back into line — what said
   "terminal choice" was never the extra eight pixels. */
.bp__mat {
    height: 34px;
    padding: 0 13px 0 11px;
    margin-right: 8px;
}

/* ---- 4b, Rail */

.bp__split { flex-direction: row; }

.bp__rail {
    width: 186px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding: 12px 10px;
    border-right-width: 1px;
    border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
}

.bp__rail-row {
    height: 34px;
    padding: 0 10px;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    border-left-width: 2px;
}

.bp__pane {
    flex-grow: 1;
    padding: 12px 14px;
}

.bp__pane-label {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
}

/* Four columns of tiles, and they are allowed to end in a ragged row rather than close up:
   this layout's whole point is that its height does not move when the category does. The grid
   is sized for the largest category and stays that size, which is the only version of that
   promise the layout keeps by itself — see HudLayout.BuildRailSubRows for the measurement that
   showed it was not being kept. */
.bp__sub-grid, .bp__mat-grid {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-content: flex-start;
}

.bp__sub-grid {
    height: 138px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

/* Reserved even while it is empty. A category whose first buildable tool is not made of anything
   hides these buttons, and a hidden row takes its height with it — which moved the panel by 27px
   and broke the one promise this layout makes. */
.bp__mat-grid {
    height: 92px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__sub-tile {
    height: 66px;
    width: 23%;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-right: 6px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
}

.bp__sub-tile .bp__tile-label {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-top: 4px;
}

.bp__mat-tile {
    height: 86px;
    width: 23%;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-right: 6px;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
}

.bp__mat-tile .bp__material-label {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-top: 6px;
}

/* ---- 4c, Bar */

.bp__bar-row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 8px 14px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.bp__bar-cat {
    width: 36px;
    height: 36px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-right: 4px;
}

.bp__bar-subs {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border-right-width: 1px;
    border-right-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.10);
    padding-right: 12px;
    margin-right: 12px;
}

.bp__bar-sub {
    width: 42px;
    height: 42px;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-right: 4px;
}

.bp__bar-mats {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

.bp__bar-hint { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50); }

/* ---- materials, in every layout

   Two pixels of border where everything else has one, because a material is the panel's terminal
   choice. The fill, the border colour and the ink are written from code: a tinted button always
   means buildable, and an untinted one never does, which is what lets the stock state be read
   without putting a number on the button. */

.bp__material {
    border-width: 2px;
    border-radius: 5px;
}

.bp__material-label {
    margin-left: 9px;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.bp__material--on {
    border-bottom-width: 4px;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- B17 settings

   Centred, and deliberately narrow: it holds a short list of switches, not a settings tree. Not a
   modal, so there is no scrim behind it and the board stays visible and running underneath, which
   is the whole reason to open it. */

.settings {
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    translate: -50% -50%;
    width: 300px;
}

/* The debug menu (backtick). Centred exactly like Settings and mutually exclusive with it
   (HudShell.Debug.cs / HudShell.Bar.cs each close the other on open), so reusing the same spot
   costs nothing and needs no second layout to judge. */
.debug {
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    translate: -50% -50%;
    width: 280px;
}

.settings__tabs {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 6px;
    height: 26px;
    border-bottom-width: 1px;
    border-bottom-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
}

.settings__body { margin-top: 9px; }

/* The scale ladder: one rung per percentage, the live one filled. Chips rather than a slider,
   because a scale rung is a choice and not a continuum — the chip is the idiom this HUD uses
   for every control that holds a choice. The faders below are the other kind. */
.settings__ladder {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 4px;
    margin-left: 26px;
}

.rung {
    height: 26px;
    padding: 0 9px;
    margin: 0 4px 4px 0;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.rung:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

.rung--on {
    background-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
    color: #0b1116;
}

/* A volume fader: the one control in this HUD that holds a continuum rather than a choice.
   Unity seated at the centre (the notch below marks it), silence at the left end, boost at
   the right — each half linear in dB over its own span. The built-in slider restyled rather
   than replaced, the Build palette's scroller having shown the way in. The tracker's end
   margins are half the thumb's width on purpose: Unity sweeps the thumb's LEFT edge from the
   track's start to its end minus the thumb, so those margins are what make the thumb's centre
   sweep exactly the track — and what puts its centre on the notch at unity. */
.settings__fader {
    height: 24px;
    margin: 0 6px 4px 26px;
}

.settings__fader .unity-base-slider__tracker {
    height: 3px;
    align-self: center;
    border-radius: 1.5px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-width: 0;
    margin-left: 6px;
    margin-right: 6px;
}

.settings__fader .unity-base-slider__dragger {
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-width: 0;
    margin-left: 0;
    top: 50%;
    margin-top: -6px;
}

.settings__fader .unity-base-slider__dragger:hover { background-color: #ffffff; }

/* Where unity sits: the centre mark the default is seated on, so putting a fader back the way
   it shipped is a place rather than a memory. First child, so the track and the thumb both
   draw over it. */
.settings__fader-notch {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: 50%;
    width: 1px;
    height: 7px;
    margin-left: -0.5px;
    margin-top: -3.5px;
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
}

/* The figure a fader rests at, beside its row's label: the saying half of the control the
   slider is the dragging half of. Wide enough for the longest figure in the mono face, so
   the label does not walk as the thumb moves. */
.settings__value {
    min-width: 52px;
    margin-left: 6px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    -unity-text-align: middle-right;
}

/* A row that only labels the control under it, and answers nothing itself. */
.settings__row--static:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }

.settings__section {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    margin: 9px 0 4px 0;
}

.settings__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 29px;
    padding: 0 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
}

.settings__row:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

.settings__label {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 9px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
    white-space: nowrap;
}

/* The switch itself: a hollow pip that fills with the accent when the lever is on. A lit chip is
   the on-off idiom the rest of this HUD uses, and the first real UI Toolkit control in the
   project should arrive with an interface pass rather than in a settings panel. */
.settings__pip {
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35);
}

.settings__row--on .settings__pip {
    background-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
}

.settings__note {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    white-space: normal;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

/* One key cap on a binding row. A small rung rather than the command bar's bare cap, because
   a cap here is a control in its own right — click it, and the next key pressed is offered to
   the slot it names. */
.settings__keycap {
    min-width: 26px;
    height: 19px;
    margin-left: 6px;
    padding: 0 6px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.settings__keycap:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

.settings__keycap--listening {
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
    color: #6fd3e3;
}

/* The reset row under the binding list, set apart from the list it undoes. */
.settings__reset { margin-top: 9px; }

/* The way out, below every tab: separated by a hairline, because it is not a setting and
   should not read as one. Armed, it says what it is waiting for and the accent says it means
   it. */
.settings__exit {
    margin-top: 9px;
    border-top-width: 1px;
    border-top-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
}

/* A row that has been pressed once and is asking to be sure. Not named for the exit row any more
   (U38): the settings panel grew Load and Quit-to-main-menu beside it, and the start screen has
   its own Quit, and all of them ask the same question in the same voice. One class, so the answer
   to "what does a row look like when it is waiting for a second press" has one place. */
.row--armed { background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.12); }

.row--armed .settings__label { color: #6fd3e3; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the modal scrim

   The third scrim, and the only one that is not always on. The two at the top and bottom of the
   screen are ramps carrying the HUD's text contrast; this one is flat, covers the viewport, and
   says that what is behind it is not available. Flat rather than a ramp, so it needs no generated
   texture and can live here as a plain fill.

   It is a pointer target on purpose, which is the whole of "a modal swallows every pointer event"
   (09-ui-and-input.md section 6, case 5): a full-viewport element under the panel and over
   everything else absorbs what would otherwise reach the world. */

.modal-scrim {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
    background-color: rgba(8, 11, 14, 0.66);
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- B18 the start screen

   The screen before the game: the only one that exists when no world is built. Centred like the
   settings panel, and for the same reason — it belongs to no button, so there is nothing to anchor
   it to. It is a window, so it takes the opaque fill and the close X from .window; the X is
   suppressed on the root screen, which has nothing to close to.

   It introduces no control of its own. A row here is a .settings__row, which is the same row the
   stores panel, the Menu popover and the settings panel are built from. */

/* A fixed box, and fixed is the point (owner, 2026-09-17: "keep it fixed width and height because
   it becomes hard to read between loading and saving screens"). It used to size itself to its
   content, so the root screen and the load screen gave two different boxes — and since the panel is
   centred, moving between them moved every row under the pointer. A menu whose items walk away as
   you navigate is a menu you have to re-find each time. */
.startscreen {
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    translate: -50% -50%;
    width: 420px;
    height: 384px;
}

/* Everything under the title. One height for every screen this panel shows, which is what makes
   the panel's own height a constant rather than a consequence. */
.startscreen__body {
    height: 318px;
    margin-top: 14px;
}

/* The game's own name, at the largest word-face step the type scale has. An explicit box with
   middle alignment, like every other label that shares a column, so its leading falls outside the
   box rather than pushing the rows below it down. */
.startscreen__title {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 26px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

/* Top-aligned in a body that is taller than they are. The air below is deliberate: the alternative
   to a fixed body is a list that scrolls at four rows. */
.startscreen__rows { flex-shrink: 0; }

/* The saves, when the load screen is showing. A max height and a scroll, which is the first
   ceiling any region in this interface has carried — and it is here first because this is the
   first region whose length is set by the player rather than by the game. The scroller itself is
   restyled by the .build__scroll rules above; this list wears the same class so there is one
   scrollbar in the project rather than two. Six rows, which is what the fixed body leaves once the
   row that goes back has taken its share. */
.startscreen__list {
    flex-grow: 0;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    min-height: 0;
    max-height: 284px;
}

.save {
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    height: 44px;
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    padding: 0 8px;
    border-radius: 3px;
}

.save:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

.save__name {
    color: #eef3f6;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* Day, map, and — for a file this build cannot open — the reason. Listed rather than hidden: a
   save that vanishes from the list is a colony the player concludes is gone. */
.save__meta {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    white-space: nowrap;
}

.save--bad { opacity: 0.42; }
.save--bad:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }
.save--bad .save__meta { color: #e8b55c; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the New game screen (U39)

   The seed, a reroll and the row that commits, in the region the load list sits in. It introduces
   no control either: a caption, the .field the naming prompt already restyled, and two
   .settings__row. The only new rule with any weight is the disabled row, which is how the screen
   says that what is in the box does not name a seed. */

/* The word over the box, in the quiet ink every figure in this interface is introduced by. */
.startscreen__seedcap {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    height: 18px;
}

/* Reroll and Start, set off from the field above them by the gap the field itself already keeps. */
.startscreen__seedrows { margin-top: 9px; }

/* A row that cannot be pressed, in the idiom .menu__row--off and .chip--off already use. It is
   drawn rather than hidden, because a Start that vanishes while you are mid-edit is a screen that
   looks broken; faint and inert says "not yet" where absent says "never". */
.settings__row--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.settings__row--off:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }

/* The debug menu's own inert-row look, identical to .settings__row--off but its own class: that
   one is queried by name in StartScreenTests to find the disabled Start row specifically, and a
   second row sharing it would make that query ambiguous the moment both panels exist at once. */
.debug__row--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.debug__row--off:hover { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the menu backdrop

   Behind everything, and only ever seen when there is no colony — with a world running the backdrop
   is the world. The picture and its scale mode are set from code, because the file is loaded from
   Resources and may not be there at all; a clone without the art simply gets the fill. */

.backdrop {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
    background-color: #05070a;
}

/* Everything that only means anything with a colony running. One container so it can be put away
   as one thing: half these regions set display in code, and an inline style beats a rule here. */
.worldui {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0; top: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the setup page

   The first thing in this project that is not a panel. The owner asked for the inspect pane's whole
   skills grid on a candidate; that grid is 900px wide, so three candidates with a detail beside them
   is not something a 420x384 box can hold. New game leaves the box rather than stretching it, and
   the box is left exactly as it is for Load and Settings. docs/design/19-world-setup.md. */

/* A panel, not a new box: the element wears .panel and .window, so the fill (rgba(12,16,20,0.96)),
   the border and the radius are the in-game menus' own tokens and nothing here restates them
   (owner, 2026-09-18: "use the same transparency/translucent as the in game menus, not to
   reinvent"). What .setup overrides is only where it sits and how much air it keeps: inset from
   every edge so the border reads as a box around the interface rather than as a screen border. */
.setup {
    position: absolute;
    left: 24px; top: 24px; right: 24px; bottom: 24px;
    padding: 32px 40px;
}

/* A section heading: "Skills", "Traits". Bigger and bolder than the block under it, and set off
   above so it belongs to what follows rather than floating between two blocks. */
.setup__heading {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 26px;
    margin-top: 22px;
}

/* Every clickable row on this page carries an outline, so what can be pressed is visible without
   hovering it (owner, 2026-09-18). Scoped to the page: .settings__row is also the settings panel's
   and the Menu popover's row, and those are read over a running world where a grid of outlines
   would be noise. */
.setup .settings__row {
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
}

.setup__title {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 26px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
}

/* The board, across the top: what it is called, what number it came from, and how big it is. */
.setup__board {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-end;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
}

.setup__field {
    flex-direction: column;
    width: 220px;
    margin-right: 18px;
}

.setup__size {
    flex-direction: column;
    width: 140px;
    margin-left: 18px;
}

.setup__sizevalue {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 30px;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.setup__size:hover .setup__sizevalue { color: #6fd3e3; }

/* A row used inline rather than in a column: Reroll beside the seed, Back and Start along the
   bottom. Same .settings__row underneath, so nothing new is introduced. */
.setup__inline {
    width: 160px;
    flex-grow: 0;
}

.setup__people {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

/* Two columns rather than however many fit the viewport (owner, 2026-09-18), and set clear of the
   record above it. The grid is a wrapping row in a pane that grows, so at 1920 it had laid thirteen
   skills out four across and filled the page edge to edge; the cap is what holds it to two and
   leaves the right-hand half for whatever comes. Derived in HudLayout.SetupSkillsWidth. */
.skills--setup {
    max-width: 636px;
    margin-top: 24px;
}

/* Wider and taller than the inspect pane's line, at a step up the type scale: this screen is read
   at leisure with no world behind it. */
.skill--setup {
    width: 300px;
    height: 24px;
}

/* The way in, and the only green row in the game. Back and Start sit side by side and read
   identically otherwise, so the colour is what says which is which.

   Written as a descendant so it out-specifies `.setup .settings__row` above, which outlines every
   pressable row on this page: that selector is 0,2,0 and a bare `.setup__commit` is 0,1,0, so the
   grey would have won and the green border would never have drawn. Specificity beats order. */
.setup .setup__commit {
    background-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.15);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.45);
    border-radius: 3px;
}

.setup .setup__commit .settings__label { color: #7fc98c; }

.setup .setup__commit:hover { background-color: rgba(127, 201, 140, 0.24); }

/* The one being read, beside the three. */
.setup__detail {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 32px;
}

/* The portrait and the record it belongs to, side by side (docs/design/20-avatars.md §3). */
.detail__record {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: flex-start;
}

.detail__lines {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 18px;
}

.detail__name { color: #eef3f6; height: 26px; }
.detail__trade { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66); height: 18px; }
/* A section under the skills now rather than a third line of the record, so it reads as the block
   M7 will fill rather than as another fact about the person (owner, 2026-09-18). */
.detail__traits { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50); height: 18px; }

.setup__footer {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 18px;
}

.colonists__rows { margin-top: 9px; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the colonist screen (U40)

   Three candidates, each a name and one line of skills. A card is clicked to keep it through a
   reroll, and a kept one wears .row--armed — the same lit treatment every "this is on" in the
   interface uses, so nothing new has to be learned. */

.colonists {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 340px;
}

/* A row wrapping a column since avatars landed (docs/design/20-avatars.md §3): the face, then the
   three lines about the person whose face it is — name, trade, and what they are good at. The same
   shape a roster card's identity row is.

   Sixty-five is the three lines, and it must not fall under the 60 px face: at 47 the faces ran
   into one another and into the selection outline (HudLayout.ColonistCard). */
.colonist {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 76px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    padding: 8px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
}

.colonist:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07); }

/* The one whose record is showing on the right. A different question from "kept", which wears
   .row--armed, so it needs a different mark: an outline rather than a fill. */
.colonist--on { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

/* The two lines beside the face. Overflow hidden so that the name, which is the one text in this
   interface allowed to be cut short, is cut by the column rather than by the screen. */
.colonist__lines {
    flex-grow: 1;
    margin-left: 8px;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* Dim rather than meta since the skills line arrived under it: three lines at two weights read as
   a hierarchy, and an occupation is flavour where the line below it is the decision. */
.colonist__trade {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.66);
    height: 20px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.colonist__name {
    color: #eef3f6;
    height: 26px;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* The row that goes back, set apart from the list above it the way the settings panel's exit row
   is set apart from the tabs. */
.startscreen__back {
    margin-top: 4px;
    border-top-width: 1px;
    border-top-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.09);
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the naming prompt

   Names a save before it is written (owner, 2026-09-17). A modal over a running colony, which is
   the first time that combination exists — the start screen's modal has no world behind it. The
   world keeps running underneath, which is the owner's standing answer for what a modal does. */

.prompt {
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    translate: -50% -50%;
    width: 420px;
}

/* The first text field in the project, so it is restyled from Unity's default rather than
   accepted — the default is a pale box with a blue focus ring, which against a near-black panel
   reads as a control from a different program. The scroller and the volume slider went the same
   way; this is the third. No font-size here: type belongs to HudType, and the sheet is checked
   for that. */
.field {
    height: 30px;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

.field .unity-base-text-field__input {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0 8px;
    color: #eef3f6;
    --unity-cursor-color: #6fd3e3;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}

.field:hover .unity-base-text-field__input { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35); }
.field:focus .unity-base-text-field__input { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

/* The two answers, side by side under the field: confirm on the left where the eye already is
   after typing, leave on the right. */
.prompt__answers {
    flex-direction: row;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

/* The answer IS its label, the way a settings rung is — one element carrying the box and the word
   together. It was a box with a Label inside it, and that Label had no class, so it took the
   imported theme's dark ink and could not be seen at all. */
.prompt__answer {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    height: 29px;
    margin-right: 4px;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    border-radius: 4px;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.82);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}

.prompt__answer:hover { border-color: #6fd3e3; }

/* Armed: the same accent wash every other row that asks twice uses. It needs its own line because
   .row--armed colours a `.settings__label` child, and this control has no child to colour. */
.prompt__answer--armed {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.12);
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
    color: #6fd3e3;
}

/* A name that cannot be written — empty, or nothing but punctuation. Disabled with its reason
   rather than a press that silently does nothing, which is the panel catalogue's rule. */
.prompt__answer--off { opacity: 0.42; }
.prompt__answer--off:hover { border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13); }

/* The line under the field: what will happen, in words, before it happens. It is the only warning
   there is, since the write itself is instant and silent. */
.prompt__note {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.50);
    white-space: normal;
    margin-top: 9px;
}

.prompt__note--warn { color: #e8b55c; }

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the drag-select marquee */

/* What the player is holding: one word, in a box whose border is the held order's own colour —
   green for chopping, blue for mining, amber for deconstructing, red for cancelling (owner,
   2026-09-17). Centred over the command bar, out of the way of the board. Never a click target.

   The border is three pixels, and the colour is written from code, because the border is the
   whole of what this says at a glance; the cyan here is the resting value for a build tool, which
   has no order hue of its own. See HudTheme.ArmedBorderWidth.

   The line under the word — "drag over the board · right-click or Esc to stop" — was removed on
   the owner's instruction the same day. It was the only place the right-click gesture was written
   down; the orders strip now lights the button that armed the tool and puts it down when pressed
   again, which is a way out you can see rather than one you have to be told. */
.armed {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    translate: -50% 0;
    bottom: 58px;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 5px 14px;
    background-color: rgba(12, 16, 20, 0.90);
    border-width: 3px;
    border-color: #6fd3e3;
    border-radius: 6px;
}

.armed__what { color: #dff6fa; }


.marquee {
    position: absolute;
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
    background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12);
}

/* The bed's owner row: the readout's one pickable fact.

   Two rounds of this were too quiet to find. First a pointer cursor and a hover brighten, on the
   argument that a row which shouted would be a button wearing a row's clothes; then a border and
   a chevron. The owner could not assign a bed either time (2026-09-17, and again after). The
   lesson is that hover is not an affordance — a control has to look like one while the pointer is
   somewhere else entirely — so the value now sits in a filled box with a bed glyph before it and
   its text in the accent colour at full weight. It is a button, and it is dressed as one.

   The box is a plain container on every other row, which is what keeps one row structure for
   facts and for the one control among them. */
.inspect__rowbox {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

.inspect__row--pick { cursor: pointer; }

.inspect__row--pick .inspect__rowbox {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.14);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.45);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding-left: 5px;
    padding-right: 4px;
    flex-grow: 0;
    align-self: center;
}

/* Weight is HudType's and not the sheet's (TheSheetSetsNoTypeAtAll): the value is re-set in the
   heavier Row role when the row becomes pickable, and the sheet keeps colour, space and state. */
.inspect__row--pick .inspect__rowvalue {
    color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
    flex-grow: 0;
    padding-left: 4px;
}

.inspect__row--pick:hover .inspect__rowbox {
    background-color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.26);
    border-color: rgb(111, 211, 227);
}

.inspect__row--pick:hover .inspect__rowvalue { color: rgb(238, 250, 253); }

.inspect__rowglyph { margin-right: 1px; }

.inspect__rowchevron {
    color: rgba(111, 211, 227, 0.75);
    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
    padding-left: 4px;
}
.inspect__row--pick:hover .inspect__rowchevron { color: rgb(238, 250, 253); }

/* The picker it raises: one row per colonist plus the release, over the row that raised it. */
.bedowner { width: 220px; }
.bedowner__rows { margin-top: 2px; }
.bedowner__row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    height: 24px;
    padding-left: 10px;
}
.bedowner__row:hover { background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05); }
.bedowner__name { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72); }
